WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.751 --> 00:00:03.280
Film Courage: When did you know you&nbsp;
were going to be a writer, Glenn?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:10.080
Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: I was really little I&nbsp;
was like nine or ten years old I actually at first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.920
I wanted to be a fictional character. I distinctly&nbsp;
remember wanting to be James Bond or The Hardy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.720
Boys or Sherlock Holmes. It was mostly detectives&nbsp;
and spies and at some point around 10 or 11 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.800
old I it occurred to me that there were people&nbsp;
who wrote those people that I wanted to be and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:36.000
that life I wanted to live and I sort of shifted&nbsp;
an inch over but I’ve never really stopped wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.400
to be fictional. That's how writing is for me,&nbsp;
it's just an attempt to be fictional at all times.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.440
Film Courage: How did you discover&nbsp;
that oh wait there are authors here?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:53.200
Glenn: There was a magazine called show&nbsp;
magazine at the back in the 1960s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.920
there was an interview with Ian Fleming who wrote&nbsp;
James Bond and he wrote an article about how he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.200
wrote James Bond and it blew my mind because&nbsp;
there was this person saying how you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.720
create this world with these people in it and&nbsp;
I was like that's what I want to be and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:17.760
Harriet The Spy was a huge influence on me&nbsp;
she wanted to be a writer and she she explored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.240
the world around her and I was like that's&nbsp;
that's what the job I want that's the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.400
did your parents try to talk you out of it or&nbsp;
were they encouraging no no my parents were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.720
supportive I was very lucky in that way they were&nbsp;
scared they were nervous but it wasn't like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:41.440
was an outrageous thing to do it was just we hope&nbsp;
that you can survive oh and and there were times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.800
when that was in question they helped me out&nbsp;
financially a lot especially in the many years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:55.280
after college when I was not making any money i've&nbsp;
been very lucky that way I had a lot of support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.320
can you explain how anxiety&nbsp;
almost ruined your writing career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:07.040
it almost stopped it from starting I was&nbsp;
a terribly terribly anxious young person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.680
still I'm very anxious I just actually writing has&nbsp;
become sort of the way I counter anxiety it's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:20.560
of the few things that's totally within my control&nbsp;
and control is what helped having the feeling of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:27.440
having something in your control is what helps&nbsp;
anxiety so being able to go into a fictional world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:34.240
or create a world or follow a story in your mind&nbsp;
was one of the few things that would prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.160
me from feeling anxious and yet when I tried to&nbsp;
write them down I was always very self-critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.840
it always seemed more complicated than just making&nbsp;
it up in your head to actually get it down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:53.760
on paper was was very difficult and so I&nbsp;
started wanting to write when I was 10 or 11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.200
and I would write outlines or plans for things and&nbsp;
write beginnings of things but I wouldn't be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.640
to actually finish them and I could not figure&nbsp;
out why the answer was anxiety the answer was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:14.240
having a process and and it took about 10 15 years&nbsp;
of struggling with that and having writer's block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.880
and and being talented I mean I could think of&nbsp;
this stuff I would write good stuff when I wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.880
but I would write very very rarely and it would&nbsp;
be very upsetting and one of the things I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:33.840
to teach myself which is what I now try to teach&nbsp;
others is the importance of having a reassuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:40.000
process a routine a very mechanical way of&nbsp;
doing the work so that there's no anxiety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.160
involved because it's just like if you were&nbsp;
a musician you would practice your instrument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:52.000
regardless of quality regardless of of importance&nbsp;
you just run your finger over the keys over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.280
over and over again and if you do that as a writer&nbsp;
if you push yourself to just write even if it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.960
bad if you push yourself to just get used to the&nbsp;
experience of stopping and starting and starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.880
and stopping every day for days and days and&nbsp;
days eventually it becomes so comforting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.880
familiar that you can actually do the creative&nbsp;
stuff the creative stuff comes after the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.960
a lot of this actually reminded me I eventually&nbsp;
ran into stanislavski's writing about actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.240
and I recognize that it's the same thing&nbsp;
I actually think of it as method writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:32.080
because the idea of an actor having to go&nbsp;
out on a stage and be able to summon up an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.280
emotion on cue every night when it was in&nbsp;
theater and you had to do it every night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.240
was a struggle that anyone would have a problem&nbsp;
doing and so the stanislavski tried to work out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:54.160
a process a method by which you can create a&nbsp;
a very mysterious thing called emotion on cue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.640
even if it's not yours even if you don't feel&nbsp;
it and that's essentially what a writer does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.680
there's just nobody looking but you have to sit&nbsp;
down at your desk and somehow transport yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.040
into a fictional world into a created place&nbsp;
with people who are not you and you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.000
feel these feelings and imagine this stuff that&nbsp;
has to come out of you from you know not where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.360
people don't know where creativity comes from you&nbsp;
know when you get it you know when it's happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:26.880
but you don't know how it happens and you can't so&nbsp;
what you can know is if I create this ritual this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.240
routine by which I'm going to always open the same&nbsp;
type of notebook or I'm going to have the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.760
format I'm working in and I'm going to understand&nbsp;
the mechanics of writing the idea of character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:43.920
and story and people taking action and if&nbsp;
you get all those things and you apply them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.760
to whatever you're writing over and over you'll&nbsp;
eventually become so comfortable with the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.600
that you'll become creative and after&nbsp;
many many years where I would just panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.520
every time I had to write now I actually find that&nbsp;
writing is the thing that keeps me from panicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:08.880
about life and that I'll find myself every&nbsp;
day if I don't write I get nervous but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:18.160
if I do write or if I if I trust the process&nbsp;
it will absolutely take away the anxiety I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.800
you can't guarantee much about the arts but&nbsp;
I can guarantee that practice and exposure to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.480
the process and creating a ritual is almost&nbsp;
always guaranteed to make your art better&nbsp;&nbsp;

